# Both spellings of the failure constructor, pinned (2026-09-06).
#
# THE QUESTION THIS ANSWERS. `spec:106` says that when two parameters in a
# signature share a type, named arguments are mandatory at the call site.
# `spec:146` presents the failure constructor as `fail(code, msg)`, and both of
# its parameters are `str` — `selfhost/check/walk.hero` checks each against
# `str_ty` and never consults a label. So a reader who applies the rule and a
# reader who copies the line write different programs, and the document does not
# say which is right.
#
# THE ANSWER IS BOTH, and it was measured rather than argued: each spelling
# below compiles and runs. That is the strongest of the three answers the
# question had, because it is the only one in which no reader can be wrong.
#
# WHY A FIXTURE AND NOT A SENTENCE IN THE SPEC. A sentence costs tokens against
# a headroom that panel 115 has just spent six of getting back, to describe a
# case where nobody can be wrong. This project's own rule is that a rule an
# instrument reads is a rule and a rule in prose is a wish — so the fact is
# pinned here, and the day either spelling stops compiling this goes red instead
# of a document going quietly stale.
#
# A user function with two `str` parameters is included on purpose: it is the
# other half of the claim, and it still DEMANDS the labels. If the two ever
# converge, one of these two functions stops compiling and says so.

function positional() -> i64?
    return fail("negative", "the document's own spelling, at spec:146")

function labelled() -> i64?
    return fail(code: "negative", msg: "the spelling spec:106's rule implies")

function joined(a: str, b: str) -> str
    return a + b

function main()
    print(positional().default(1))
    print(labelled().default(2))
    print(joined(a: "the rule ", b: "still binds a user function"))

# Since 2026-09-10 the specification's sections are numbered and a citation names
# the section, never a line (CL-037). The line numbers above were written against
# the layout at commit 834d804f; read today they are:
#   spec:106 is spec § 9 Functions and calls` says that when two parameters in a
#   spec:146 is spec § 6 Failure` presents the failure constructor as `fail(code
